Relevant columns & their potential usage for the requested analysis  

• "geo_id" (all three tract tables) : unique tract identifier; the first two‑digits are the state FIPS code used to join to CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CYCLISTIC.STATE_FIPS.  
• "unemployed_pop" (2017) : piece of the adjusted non‑labor‑force formula.  
• "not_in_labor_force" (2017) : piece of the adjusted non‑labor‑force formula.  
• "group_quarters" (2017) : subtract from the sum above when building the adjusted figure.  
• "total_pop" (2017) : denominator to create tract‑level proportions.  
• "median_income" (2015 & 2018) : used to compute the change in median household income between the two years.  
• "fips" (STATE_FIPS) : state code to join to the left‑most two digits of "geo_id".  
• "postal_code" (STATE_FIPS) : requested state abbreviation to display.  

Typical conditions we will need  

• WHERE "median_income" IS NOT NULL on both the 2015 & 2018 tables before taking a difference.  
• WHERE "total_pop" > 0 to avoid divide‑by‑zero when computing proportions.  
• We will usually CAST/LTRIM/LPAD the "fips" to two characters to compare to SUBSTR("geo_id",1,2).  
• In the final step we will order by the aggregated adjusted non‑labor‑force population (ascending) and LIMIT 3.  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (all use LIMIT 20 for sampling) that build up from simple examination of columns to the combined aggregation required.  
All identifiers are wrapped in double quotes exactly as they appear in the metadata.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Inspect unemployed, not‑in‑labor‑force, and group‑quarters counts for a few 2017 tracts
SELECT 
  "geo_id",
  "unemployed_pop",
  "not_in_labor_force",
  "group_quarters"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Compute the raw adjusted non‑labor‑force value for a sample and check if any come out negative
SELECT 
  "geo_id",
  "unemployed_pop" + "not_in_labor_force" - "group_quarters" AS "raw_adjusted_nlf"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Look at NULL distribution for 2015 median income
SELECT 
  COUNT(*) AS "tracts_2015",
  SUM(CASE WHEN "median_income" IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "null_median_income_2015"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2015_5YR";
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Look at NULL distribution for 2018 median income
SELECT 
  COUNT(*) AS "tracts_2018",
  SUM(CASE WHEN "median_income" IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS "null_median_income_2018"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2018_5YR";
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Display the distinct two‑digit prefixes of geo_id and how many tracts each covers (2017 sample)
SELECT 
  SUBSTR("geo_id",1,2) AS "state_fips_prefix",
  COUNT(*)                AS "tract_count"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR"
GROUP BY 1
ORDER BY 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Show the mapping between prefixes in tract data and state abbreviations
SELECT 
  s."postal_code",
  s."fips",
  SUBSTR(t."geo_id",1,2) AS "prefix"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CYCLISTIC."STATE_FIPS"  s
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR"  t
      ON LPAD(s."fips",2,'0') = SUBSTR(t."geo_id",1,2)
GROUP BY s."postal_code", s."fips", SUBSTR(t."geo_id",1,2)
ORDER BY s."postal_code"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Compute median income change for a small sample of tracts
SELECT 
  t17."geo_id",
  t18."median_income" - t15."median_income" AS "income_change"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR" t17
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2015_5YR" t15 
     ON t17."geo_id" = t15."geo_id"
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2018_5YR" t18
     ON t17."geo_id" = t18."geo_id"
WHERE t18."median_income" IS NOT NULL
  AND t15."median_income" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Bring everything together for sample tracts – adjusted NLF value, proportion, and income change
SELECT 
  t17."geo_id",
  GREATEST(0, t17."unemployed_pop" + t17."not_in_labor_force" - t17."group_quarters")      AS "adjusted_nlf",
  t17."total_pop",
  GREATEST(0, t17."unemployed_pop" + t17."not_in_labor_force" - t17."group_quarters") / NULLIF(t17."total_pop",0) AS "adjusted_prop",
  t18."median_income" - t15."median_income"                                                 AS "income_change"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR" t17
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2015_5YR" t15 ON t17."geo_id" = t15."geo_id"
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2018_5YR" t18 ON t17."geo_id" = t18."geo_id"
WHERE t18."median_income" IS NOT NULL
  AND t15."median_income" IS NOT NULL
  AND t17."total_pop" > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Aggregate by state – sum adjusted NLF, average proportion, sum of income change
SELECT 
  s."postal_code",
  SUM(GREATEST(0, t17."unemployed_pop" + t17."not_in_labor_force" - t17."group_quarters"))                           AS "total_adjusted_nlf",
  AVG( GREATEST(0, t17."unemployed_pop" + t17."not_in_labor_force" - t17."group_quarters") / NULLIF(t17."total_pop",0) )  AS "avg_adjusted_prop",
  SUM( t18."median_income" - t15."median_income" )                                                                     AS "total_income_change"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR" t17
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2015_5YR" t15  ON t17."geo_id" = t15."geo_id"
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2018_5YR" t18  ON t17."geo_id" = t18."geo_id"
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CYCLISTIC."STATE_FIPS"                     s   ON LPAD(s."fips",2,'0') = SUBSTR(t17."geo_id",1,2)
WHERE t18."median_income" IS NOT NULL
  AND t15."median_income" IS NOT NULL
  AND t17."total_pop" > 0
GROUP BY s."postal_code"
ORDER BY "total_adjusted_nlf"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final query (top‑3 states with smallest total adjusted NLF, showing all requested fields)
SELECT 
  s."postal_code"                                                               AS "state_abbrev",
  SUM( t18."median_income" - t15."median_income" )                              AS "total_median_income_change",
  SUM( GREATEST(0, t17."unemployed_pop" + t17."not_in_labor_force" - t17."group_quarters") ) AS "total_adjusted_non_labor_force",
  AVG( GREATEST(0, t17."unemployed_pop" + t17."not_in_labor_force" - t17."group_quarters") / NULLIF(t17."total_pop",0) )  AS "avg_adjusted_prop"
FROM CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2017_5YR" t17
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2015_5YR" t15  ON t17."geo_id" = t15."geo_id"
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS."CENSUSTRACT_2018_5YR" t18  ON t17."geo_id" = t18."geo_id"
JOIN CENSUS_BUREAU_ACS_2.CYCLISTIC."STATE_FIPS"                     s   ON LPAD(s."fips",2,'0') = SUBSTR(t17."geo_id",1,2)
WHERE t18."median_income" IS NOT NULL
  AND t15."median_income" IS NOT NULL
  AND t17."total_pop" > 0
GROUP BY s."postal_code"
ORDER BY "total_adjusted_non_labor_force" ASC
LIMIT 3;
```

These ten queries progressively validate column contents, build required intermediate calculations, and culminate in the final aggregation that fulfills the stated analytic task.
